<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Émile Zola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1840">1840</a>–<a href="/years/1902">1902</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Émile Édouard Charles Antoine Zola, Zola</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Émile Édouard Charles Antoine Zola was a French novelist, journalist and playwright, the best-known practitioner of the literary school of naturalism. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix-en-Provence</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-cezanne">Paul Cézanne</a> was his childhood friend, he worked as a clerk and for the publisher Hachette before his novel Thérèse Raquin (1867). More than half of his novels belong to the twenty-volume Les Rougon-Macquart cycle, the history of one family under Napoléon III. His newspaper opinion J'Accuse...! helped exonerate the falsely convicted officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dreyfus">Alfred Dreyfus</a>. He died of carbon monoxide poisoning in 1902.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emile-zola.jpg" alt="Émile Zola" width="330" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nadar_(atelier_de)_-_Emile_Zola,_13-556535.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Émile Zola was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1840 to François Zola, originally Francesco Zolla, an Italian engineer born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1795 who built the Zola Dam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix-en-Provence</a>, and Émilie Aubert, who was French. The family moved to Aix when he was three, and his father died in 1847, leaving his mother on a small pension. In 1858 the family moved to Paris, where his childhood friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-cezanne">Paul Cézanne</a> soon joined him. His mother had planned a legal career for him, but he failed the baccalauréat examination twice.</p><p>He worked as a clerk and then for the publisher Hachette. As a political journalist he did not hide his dislike of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>. Hachette dismissed him after his autobiographical novel La Confession de Claude (1865) attracted police attention.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Thérèse Raquin (1867) Zola began Les Rougon-Macquart, which he had planned in full from the start, at the age of 28. Its twenty novels follow the respectable Rougons and the disreputable Macquarts over five generations, roughly between 1851 and 1871, and contain over 300 characters. L'Assommoir (1877) made him wealthy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a> (1880) and La Débâcle (1892) sold even better.</p><p>Zola published J'Accuse...! on the front page of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> daily L'Aurore on 13 January 1898, as an open letter to President Félix Faure accusing the highest levels of the army of obstructing justice and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a>. He intended to be prosecuted for libel so that the new evidence for Dreyfus would be made public. He was convicted on 23 February and removed from the Legion of Honour, and when a second trial opened on 18 July he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, returning in June 1899, after the Supreme Court annulled Dreyfus's verdict. Dreyfus was fully exonerated by the Supreme Court in 1906.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Zola died on 29 September 1902 of carbon monoxide poisoning caused by an improperly ventilated chimney; thousands attended his funeral on 5 October, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dreyfus">Alfred Dreyfus</a>. His enemies were blamed at the time, though nothing could be proven, and in 1953 a journalist, Jean Bedel, reported a claim that a chimney-sweep had deliberately blocked the chimney.</p><p>On 4 June 1908 his remains were moved from the Cimetière de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montmartre">Montmartre</a> to the Panthéon, where he shares a crypt with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas">Alexandre Dumas</a>. During the ceremony Dreyfus was wounded in the arm in an assassination attempt by the journalist Louis Grégori. Zola's open letter is widely viewed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as the most prominent sign of the new power of intellectuals in shaping public opinion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Émile Zola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Paris led a new consumer culture: department stores such as Le Bon Marché and La Samaritaine sold at fixed prices, and <b>Émile Zola</b> set Au Bonheur des Dames in one.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I repeat with more vehement certainty; truth is on the march, and nothing can stop it.&#8221;</p><footer>&quot;J'accuse…!&quot;, open letter to President Félix Faure, L'Aurore, 13 January 1898 &#183; tr. in The Trial of Emile Zola (Benj. R. Tucker), 1898 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/68561/pg68561-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When truth is buried in the earth, it accumulates there, and assumes so mighty an explosive power that, on the day when it bursts forth, it hurls everything into the air.&#8221;</p><footer>&quot;J'accuse…!&quot;, open letter to President Félix Faure, L'Aurore, 13 January 1898 &#183; tr. in The Trial of Emile Zola (Benj. R. Tucker), 1898 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/68561/pg68561-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
